{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\ntests/unit/test_network_fetch_stall_abort_851e.py::test_init_fetch_dribbling_remote_is_not_aborted failed during the full suite when Git measured less than 1000 bytes/sec over the test-only 2-second window. The unchanged test passed immediately afterward. The shared server in tests/_stall_remote.py currently sends a fixed rate // 10 bytes and then sleeps 0.1 seconds; every delayed wake-up permanently reduces cumulative throughput.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Quantify the existing fixed-sleep drift with a deterministic pacing test and a controlled contention reproduction.\n2. Change only the shared test server dribble scheduler to derive bytes owed from elapsed monotonic time. After a delayed wake-up it catches up to the configured cumulative byte budget instead of treating the delay as lost sending time.\n3. Exercise both consumers of the shared fixture: all stall/dribble cases in tests/unit/test_network_fetch_stall_abort_851e.py and tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py. Keep zero-byte stall mode unchanged.\n4. Verify src/rebar/_snapshot/git_fetch.py has no diff, so the 1000 B/s production floor and 10-second production window remain unchanged.\n\nThis changes the fixture pacing invariant rather than merely raising its rate or widening a finite timing margin.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A deterministic test proves delayed scheduler wake-ups no longer reduce cumulative dribble throughput.\n- [ ] Under controlled contention, all dribble cases in both consumer modules remain non-stall failures.\n- [ ] All zero-byte stall cases in both consumer modules still fail specifically as stalls.\n- [ ] git diff origin/main -- src/rebar/_snapshot/git_fetch.py is empty; production stall behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe full suite passed 11,881 tests and failed only the named dribble test with Operation too slow. Less than 1000 bytes/sec transferred the last 2 seconds; an unchanged isolated rerun passed."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786446361613684001,"uuid":"2438cf77-2176-48ec-9a28-e7a27edc1bd4"}